#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176919} :activity/id #uuid "dd162030-5f70-5928-b8cc-6898f8959595" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048526 :topic/id #uuid "a6e10c0a-ff9a-58ae-92a9-a1da0836341f" :topic/math-academy-id 3657} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176919 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177019 :tutorial/content "The fundamental theorem of algebra tells us that any quartic polynomial with real coefficients has precisely four roots. \n\nMoreover, since complex roots always come in complex conjugate pairs, if $p(x)$ is a quartic polynomial with real coefficients, we must have one of the following cases:\n\n- $p(x)$ has $4$ real roots (some of which might be repeated).\n\n- $p(x)$ has $2$ (possibly repeated) real roots and $2$ complex conjugate roots. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & ={x}^{4}-1 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-1)({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(x-1)(x+1)(x-\\text{i})(x+\\text{i}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the real roots are $x=\\pm 1,$ and the complex conjugate roots are $x=\\pm\\textrm i.$\n\n- $p(x)$ has two pairs of complex conjugate roots. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =({x}^{2}+1)({x}^{2}+4) \\\\  & =(x-\\text{i})(x+\\text{i})(x-\\text{2}\\text{i})(x+\\text{2}\\text{i}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the complex conjugate pairs of roots are $x=\\pm\\textrm i$ and $x=\\pm 2\\textrm i.$ \n\n Also, notice that it's possible for the complex conjugate pairs of roots to be repeated. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & ={x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)^{2} \\\\  & ={((x-\\text{i})(x+\\text{i}))}^{2} \\\\  & =(x-\\text{i})(x-\\text{i})(x+\\text{i})(x+\\text{i}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the roots are $x=\\pm\\textrm i.$ Each of these roots is a double root." :tutorial/id #uuid "e5351df4-0c57-5a7a-96da-a70b16eecb4e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5613 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "09f0a9f1-af6c-5787-afba-a5acbe78ce00" :step/math-academy-id 521904 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176920}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176920 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176079 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175813 :question/id #uuid "d8bf44c3-384b-553a-931f-a09ac2a3a649" :question/math-academy-id "e-8802"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57987f7d-21e1-51cb-a745-a24f2b257f11" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050439}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299917}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding All the Roots of a Quartic Equation Given Two Real Roots"} :step/id #uuid "506b374c-6541-5d8b-897c-999bd4b3b87c" :step/math-academy-id 521905 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176921}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176921 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176080 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175788 :question/id #uuid "b4649e26-6d7b-5c3e-9c8e-d55a9db5eb29" :question/math-academy-id "e-5797"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7ca77fd-e6ef-568f-be32-e1ae47c3d8dc" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299921}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding All the Roots of a Quartic Equation Given One Complex Root"} :step/id #uuid "4e182e94-572f-557b-97a4-59db500f8f68" :step/math-academy-id 521908}] :activity/title "Solving Quartic Equations With Complex Roots" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176165}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116133} :activity/id #uuid "d6f7c63b-f172-5766-b56a-db8bf2a22464" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048920 :topic/id #uuid "cc887b1d-8841-507d-a99b-b7888c08a675" :topic/math-academy-id 1728} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116133 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116498 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the inverse of the matrix \n\n![](images/fd/fd9e9dc44e27810ef490a898a7c82ae9296eaf85ac9406cf310afe86638f71e1.svg) \n\nWe can compute the inverse of $A$ using elementary row operations. There are two key advantages to using row operations to compute the inverse of a matrix:\n\n- The method works for any invertible matrix of any size, not just $2\\times 2$ matrices.\n\n- It requires significantly less computation than some alternative methods. This is especially true for large matrices.\n\nHere, we'll focus on the $2\\times 2$ case. We use the following fact:\n\n*The row operations that transform $A$ into the identity matrix $I_2$ also transform $I_2$ into $A^{-1}$*.\n\nSo, to find the inverse of $A,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1:** Write down $\\color{blue}A$ and the identity matrix $I_2$ side-by-side, as shown below:\n![](images/e5/e553bcc265698432452c8d35b79fe9dc70c25010a0bcc6874ad0302ea7033ed7.svg)\n\n**Step 2:** Apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,{I}_{2}\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\,I\\,|\\,{A}^{-1}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n![](images/b7/b7c08e9a28ebfe456a9d77ccdaf6688893dfd04ba755a989a417c8a43e80ee0c.svg)\n\nWe can easily check our result by computing $AA^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{A}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fc38ae42-2cd4-53df-9ad5-568d8e3b5fd4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3167 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d8efc5ed-eaaf-514d-b03a-248df406a7d5" :step/math-academy-id 17230 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116136}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116134 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115249 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115078 :question/id #uuid "7c480d6a-93a1-56ec-a75c-56402e872c92" :question/math-academy-id "e-4723"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f42188-bf51-5546-89fb-e33ba47c4b5e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401995}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Row Operations Required To Find the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "e874c19f-8e51-5ff3-b8d2-dd1a0540bbc2" :step/math-academy-id 17231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116135 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115250 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115079 :question/id #uuid "e13311fb-21de-58d4-aed0-18304b48bb0c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4724"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a4b135f-df0d-5443-bb93-796f32c380c6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048742}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417379}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Calculated Using Row Operations"} :step/id #uuid "7cf9141d-1c90-5675-a333-b0aa4e0f3cef" :step/math-academy-id 17232 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116134}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116136 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115251 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115080 :question/id #uuid "ff4b4389-d8ff-5b48-904b-ff405abb4e48" :question/math-academy-id "e-4725"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048252}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417378}] :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"} :step/id #uuid "e2dcd832-c069-5309-a7a3-27850adf28e2" :step/math-academy-id 17233 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116135}}] :activity/title "Finding the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Using Row Operations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115474}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116304} :activity/id #uuid "ec8f8b4c-f624-58ca-8d08-26dd958651aa" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049186 :topic/id #uuid "e1c10e80-3811-5fcd-98a3-8118c49347e4" :topic/math-academy-id 1737} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116304 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116549 :tutorial/content "The domain of a vector-valued function $\\mathbf f(t)$ is the set of values of $t$ for which the function is well-defined. \n\nA function is well-defined at some value $t = t_0$ if all its component functions are defined at $t_0.$ Thus, the domain of $\\mathbf f$ is the *intersection* of the domains of its component functions.\n\nLet's find the domain of the following function:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t) = \\dfrac{1}{3+t} \\: \\mathbf i + t^2 \\: \\mathbf j + \\sqrt{-t} \\: \\mathbf k\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the domains of the component functions:\n\n- The domain of $\\dfrac{1}{3+t}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,-3) \\cup (-3,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of $t^2$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of $\\sqrt{-t}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty, 0].$\n\nTo find the domain of $\\mathbf{f},$ we need the intersection of the component functions' domains. This intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/7c/7cf7270cc873e9de1fedb7bd413bba1f9ed1f7cd604b3ae640d32d9e0b41bdef.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,-3) \\cup (-3,0].$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d01e4be3-25c3-5922-9f88-c8eb7538e62e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7628 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6c2c6552-011f-50fb-b06a-06bc2262ccf3" :step/math-academy-id 44256 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116305}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116305 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115354 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114917 :question/id #uuid "9a86e942-8852-5cce-b9be-e7a5eff46d12" :question/math-academy-id "e-12350"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "461c0f55-cb85-511e-b8cb-be11f8a3c250" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050642}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365178}] :knowledge-point/title "Domains of Functions With Reciprocal and Radical Components"} :step/id #uuid "a07d4a92-4626-5d50-92cc-ea8d55c5707d" :step/math-academy-id 44257 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116306}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116306 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115355 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114918 :question/id #uuid "726fa7f3-a110-5730-a734-1019fd77de9a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12362"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a1499c-080d-5008-bcc3-492ef469d3dc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050829}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281523} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420092}] :knowledge-point/title "Domains of Functions With Exponential and Logarithmic Components"} :step/id #uuid "cfc9c5f1-5579-598f-a9e2-d23b659763f7" :step/math-academy-id 44297 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116307}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116307 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115356 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114919 :question/id #uuid "a63854e6-412b-5308-be28-c05337aa4b9b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12455"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "553f7b9d-c387-59b0-a0d6-9624d012e414" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049372}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398576} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420093}] :knowledge-point/title "Domains of Functions With Trigonometric Components"} :step/id #uuid "3947d2bf-1e5f-5028-8a1b-43cfaacc4546" :step/math-academy-id 44801}] :activity/title "The Domain of a Vector-Valued Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115482}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411595} :activity/id #uuid "96cfc23a-e860-5cec-be62-9f9b3c48eb57" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049186 :topic/id #uuid "e1c10e80-3811-5fcd-98a3-8118c49347e4" :topic/math-academy-id 1737} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411591 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfa1654c-e948-5fc3-a89a-4fe52cc8fccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi n}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398578} {:answer/id #uuid "8d1b7514-50b8-5ddd-9dc3-5b90007cd7cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi n}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398579} {:answer/id #uuid "61124a49-e750-5eed-9f63-4fab70f48c25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398580} {:answer/id #uuid "282a04d7-5121-553a-8c70-66602f8c83db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi n}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398581} {:answer/id #uuid "4a1c8270-8fd2-50fc-8447-30acc60e9312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi n}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398582}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398578} :answer-field/id #uuid "4112b8f8-a1d3-5ca1-a272-50f00bc81e5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b2987e81-f85b-5262-9ea2-835ea44d86e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-162644" :question/problem "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=(t+1)\\,i+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3t)\\,j+{e}^{-t}\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne .\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $t+1$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3t)$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ except for the values of $t$ for which\n\n$$\n3t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi n}{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component ${e}^{-t}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi n}{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer."} :step/id #uuid "3ce5a87f-f4ad-59bf-8bf7-3a76cd4d166e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411594}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411592 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281524 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d759664-71cf-5524-8292-b4f9f56698b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t\\in (-1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279292} {:answer/id #uuid "839d4983-b3e1-5df2-929b-e864ccf4fcda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t\\in (-1,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279769} {:answer/id #uuid "8ed76513-0186-5831-b557-a4f08af7383f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t\\in (-1,0]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279863} {:answer/id #uuid "ad393dc1-dc55-5